Answer: A class is a blueprint or template used to create objects. A class defines the properties and behaviors that its objects will have. Example:

```java
class Dog {
String name;
int age;
```

void bark() {

```java
System.out.println("Dog is barking");
}
}
```

In this example, Dog is a class. It contains two properties, name and age, and one behavior, bark().

Answer: Properties represent the data or characteristics of an object. In Java, properties are generally declared using variables. Behaviors represent the actions performed by an object. In Java, behaviors are declared using methods. Example:

```java
class Student {
String name;
int age;
```

void study() {

```java
System.out.println("Student is studying");
}
}
```

Here: name and age are properties. study() is a behavior.

Answer: A constructor is a special block in a class that is used to initialize an object. A constructor: Has the same name as the class. Does not have a return type. Is automatically called when an object is created. Initializes the object. Example:

```java
class Dog {
```

Dog() {

```java
System.out.println("Dog object created");
}
}
```

When the object is created:

```java
Dog d1 = new Dog();
```

The constructor executes automatically.

Answer: A static block is a block declared using the static keyword. It is executed once when the class is initialized by the JVM. Example:

```java
class Demo {
static {
System.out.println("Static block executed");
}
}
```

The static block is executed before the object is created, provided the class is initialized during that process.

Answer: An instance initialization block is a block that executes whenever an object is created. It executes before the constructor body. Example:

```java
class Demo {
{
System.out.println("Instance block executed");
}
```

Demo() {

```java
System.out.println("Constructor executed");
}
}
```

Output when an object is created: Instance block executed Constructor executed

Answer: The execution order is: Static block Instance initialization block Constructor Example:

```java
class Demo {
static {
System.out.println("Static block");
}
{
System.out.println("Instance block");
}
```

Demo() {

```java
System.out.println("Constructor");
}
public static void main(String[] args) {
Demo d1 = new Demo();
}
}
```

Output: Static block Instance block Constructor The static block executes only once, while the instance block and constructor execute every time an object is created.

Answer: The dot operator is used to access the properties and methods of an object. Example:

```java
class Dog {
String name;
```

void bark() {

```java
System.out.println("Dog is barking");
}
}
Dog d1 = new Dog();
d1.name = "Tommy";
d1.bark();
```

Here, the dot operator is used to access: The name property. The bark() method.

Answer: Yes, we can create multiple objects from one class. Example:

```java
class Dog {
String name;
```

void bark() {

```java
System.out.println(name + " is barking");
}
}
public class Main {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Dog d1 = new Dog();
Dog d2 = new Dog();
d1.name = "Tommy";
d2.name = "Rocky";
d1.bark();
d2.bark();
}
}
```

Output: Tommy is barking Rocky is barking Each object has its own copy of instance variables.

Answer: A class is a blueprint or template. An object is an actual instance created from that class. For example:

```java
class Dog {
String name;
}
```

Here, Dog is the class.

```java
Dog d1 = new Dog();
```

Here, d1 refers to the object created from the Dog class.

Answer: No, a static method cannot directly access instance variables because static methods belong to the class, while instance variables belong to objects. Example:

```java
class Demo {
int a = 10;
static void display() {
// System.out.println(a); // Error
}
}
```

To access an instance variable inside a static method, we must create an object.

```java
class Demo {
int a = 10;
static void display() {
Demo d1 = new Demo();
System.out.println(d1.a);
}
}
```

Answer: Local variables are generally stored in the stack frame of the method in which they are declared. Example:

```java
public static void main(String[] args) {
int age = 25;
}
```

Here, age is a local variable and is stored in the stack frame of the main() method.

